# BRIEF B1492 - SEAT-N123: NEXUSAI RD-407 ROUND 2 TEST - THE THREE PAST-BUDGET CELLS (M2, R1, R3) KEEP THE SETTINGS NAME MISSING FOR THE WHOLE CALL (COUNTED, NOT TIMED); ONE TEST FILE, ONE COMMIT; PROVE IT TIMING-PROOF (20/20 PLAIN, 20/20 UNDER A 3x SLOW-SLEEP SHIM THAT TURNS THE OLD CELLS RED), NOT WEAKENED (PLANTED PRODUCT MUTATIONS TURN IT RED), AND GREEN IN A FULL VERIFY ON THE BRANCH MERGED WITH MAIN; ONE PUSH OF A NEW BRANCH; NO PR

**Level: Sonnet.** Model-routing row **C2** ("Multi-file build or feature that fails the local predicate, not decision-shaped, not auth-design and not a destroying path", `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/TUESDAY/2_Project_Files/fleet/specs/model-routing.md:92`, sha256 prefix `5ccdffdf183e`; P4 rules out the local tier for Datasec work, `:50`). Why not C3 (`:93`, Opus): no design decision is made on the auth surface; the product is untouched and the fix shape is decided at source below. The auth weight sits in the GATE: the cells pin a fail-closed auth decision, so the gate after you is Opus (G2's "1st if the follow-up's mechanism sits on a G1 surface", `:109`). Your own guard against weakening the test is the mutation step, which is mandatory. Model `claude-sonnet-5-5` (`/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/TUESDAY/0_Brain/dashboard/data/models_latest.json`, checked 2026-11-11 07:13:31), launched with `BRIEF_SEAT_LEVEL=sonnet` written inside the launch line's quotes. Pane `Datasec/NexusAI-N123`.

## WHY (RELAYED; read at Step 0 whole: `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/TUESDAY/2_Project_Files/tuesday/briefs/2026-11-11_RD407_DIAGNOSIS.md`, 95 lines, `7942bc7b2a95`)
`__tests__/rd407-r2-missing-mid-swap.test.js` cells M2, R1 and R3 fake "settings.json is missing" for a fixed WALL-CLOCK window, `missingName(<file>, { forMs: PAST_BUDGET_MS })` with `PAST_BUDGET_MS = BUDGET + 400` = 910 ms (`:45`, `:245`, `:319`, `:344` at `0ab365b1`, `git grep -n -i`, MEASURED-BY-DRAFTER). The product retries a missing name with eight `Atomics.wait` sleeps of 2..256 ms, 510 ms nominal (`backend/jsonStorage.js:536`, `:543-545`, loop `:2991` at `0ab365b1`). Since this Mac's reboot on 2026-11-11 its sleeps overshoot about 2x, so the retries outlive the 910 ms window, the last retry reads the real store, and the cells receive `resolved` / `answer: true, gate: require-session` instead of `STORE_UNREADABLE` / `deny-unknown`. Every failure is toward the real data and fail-closed; hosted CI passes. Main's own tree failed M2/R1/R3 3 of 3 at 12:50 (RELAYED, `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/NexusAI/1_Project_Definition/Briefs/2026-11-11_B1484_STATUS.md`, section "Chain log: row 6 csp"). It blocks the landing chain because `npm run verify -- --update-counts` writes the counts file only on a fully green run. The suite's own M3/M4 cells already state the rule this kit applies: "Counted, not timed - a stall on a loaded machine is not a retry" (`:263-264`, `:288-290`).

## THE DESIGN (decided at source by the drafter; every reason is measured or marked)
1. **M2, R1, R3: the name stays missing for the WHOLE call, then `miss.restore()`.** Write `{ forMs: Infinity }` explicitly (the helper's defaults are already `observations = Infinity, forMs = Infinity`, `:128`; with `forMs: Infinity` the window test `Date.now() - startedAt > forMs` at `:138` is never true). R3 keeps `armed: false` and its `getSetting` arming. On a host whose sleeps are nominal this is the same scenario as before: the pre-reboot runs took 615-759 ms per cell (RELAYED, diagnosis Evidence 1), inside the old 910 ms window, so the name never came back during the call there either. A counted window (`observations: N`) is NOT used: N would have to exceed one plus the schedule plus every evidence read, a number the product can change under the test.
2. **M2's "spent the budget" check becomes counted.** Today it is `elapsed >= BUDGET` from `Date.now()` (`:246`, `:253`, `:255`). Replace it with the sum of the timeouts the product itself passed to `Atomics.wait` (a pass-through `jest.spyOn(Atomics, 'wait')`, restored in `finally`, exactly as M3/M4 spy at `:269`, `:292`): `spentTheBudget: requestedMs >= BUDGET`. Same assertion object and expected value, so the cell's shape does not change. Why: the wall-clock form cannot fail on a slow host and cannot see a loop cut short on a fast one; the counted form fails deterministically if the loop asks for less than the budget (plant P3 below proves it). The product's own `waitedMs` is also the sum of requested delays (`jsonStorage.js:2993`), so the test now measures what the product calls its budget.
3. **`PAST_BUDGET_MS` is deleted** (no other use: 4 hits, all listed above). `BUDGET` stays (M2 uses it). No linter exists (`package.json:27` prints "no linter configured"), but an unused constant is still removed.
4. **Every `test(` and `describe(` title line stays byte-identical**, so the suite keeps 17 cells with the same names and the counts file does not change (proved four ways, COUNTS below).
5. **Header comment** (`:13-20`) and the three cells' `// FAIL:` comments say the new model in words: "a name missing for the whole call stands for a store that really went missing; counted, not timed, because a wall-clock window shorter than the product's real retry time let the last retry read the real store on a host whose timers overshoot". Plain ASCII in every added line; no build, seat, card or person id.
6. **Out of scope, said so in READY:** product hardening (a monotonic deadline for the retry loop and an honest `waitedMs`, diagnosis "Recommended fix", product part) is a separate, optional ticket, not a security fix. If it is ever built, M2's counted check and M3/M4 must be revisited with it (they all count `Atomics.wait`).

